WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:09.180
How the heck does that work?!

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:13.020
Hey Crazies.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:16.700
If you’ve ever looked through polarized
sunglasses, you might have noticed some weird stuff

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:18.960
Like a difficulty seeing your phone screen.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.680
I’m sure your reaction was a lot like Question
Clone’s.

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:22.880
What the Fraunhofer?!?!

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:26.480
I’ve got an answer for you and, as usual,
there will be lots of details.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:28.620
Because asylum crazies think deep!

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:34.079
The lenses in polarized sunglasses are coated
in a material called a polarizer because it

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:35.420
polarizes light.

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:39.760
Scientists tend to name things more descriptively
than creatively.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.740
Anyway, this leaves us with a logical place
to start:

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:45.080
What does it mean to polarize light?

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:47.480
To answer that, we need to review a little.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:51.640
Light is often shown to be an electromagnetic
wave like this.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:56.079
Since the electric part has more influence
over things, we tend to focus more attention

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:56.920
on that.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:59.780
We call that direction the wave’s polarization.

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:02.440
What do you mean it has influence over things?

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:03.460
What things?

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:04.400
Atoms.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.060
Well, parts of atoms.

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:09.360
Here’s the train of thought:
Atoms are made of charges.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.260
Charges respond to electric fields.

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:14.820
Light has an electric field.

00:01:14.829 --> 00:01:19.079
Therefore, by the transitive property, atoms
respond to light.

00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:23.930
We’ve been talking a lot lately about electrons
absorbing light and jumping to higher energy

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:24.920
levels.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:28.840
In a solid, that extra energy is not
likely to be re-emitted as light.

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:33.980
It’s far more likely to be transferred to
nearby atoms making them jiggle faster,

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:35.860
warming up the solid instead.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:37.960
Polarizers are more like glass.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:39.400
They transmit visible light.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:40.740
They don’t hold onto it.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:43.440
Their atoms don’t have the right energy
levels for that.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:47.780
However, by the argument we just made, the
atoms should still respond to the

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:49.400
light somehow.

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:51.540
Let’s take a closer look at this glass.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:52.980
SUPER ZOOM!!!

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:57.800
This silicon atom is made of a nucleus surrounded
by a cloud of electrons.

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:03.160
The nucleus is positively charged and the
cloud negatively charged, so they’ll respond

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:04.620
in opposite directions.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:09.180
Now say some light comes in with an electric
field or polarization like this.

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:14.120
The nucleus will move with this and the cloud
of electrons will move against it.

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:19.440
Since the nucleus is 1000s of times more massive
than the cloud, the cloud’s motion dominates.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.380
But that motion is accelerated and accelerating
charges emit light.

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:27.670
The motion of that electron cloud will generate
its own light and transmit that light out

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:29.080
the back side of the glass.

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:33.100
The outgoing light is polarized along the
direction the cloud was vibrating,

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:35.600
which is the same direction as the incoming light.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:37.600
Polarizers work a little differently.

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:38.750
SUPER ZOOM!

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:41.720
The molecules in a polarizer form long chains.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:45.660
Those chains are tightly bound which limits
motion along the chain.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:49.620
If we send in the same light as before, something
different happens.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:54.640
The cloud of electrons vibrates significantly
more perpendicular to the chain,

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:57.680
even if that doesn’t match the polarization of the light.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:01.540
We call that perpendicular direction the polarizer’s
axis.

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:05.930
The motion of that electron cloud will still
generate its own light and still transmit

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:08.080
that light out the back side of the material.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:12.560
That outgoing light must still be polarized
along the direction of the wiggle,

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:14.240
but now that's a new direction.

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:16.120
There’s just one weird problem left to address.

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:20.460
The light that comes from a lightbulb or
the Sun is said to be unpolarized.

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:21.880
UNPOLARIZED!?!?!

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:24.530
How can an EM wave NOT have an electric part?

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:26.220
How is that even possible!?!

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:30.280
Because “light” and a “light beam”
are not the same thing.

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:32.700
This is not a beam of light.

00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:37.490
A beam is actually a large collection of EM
waves, so it can have different properties

00:03:37.490 --> 00:03:39.300
than a single EM wave.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:42.780
Polarization is just an example of one of
those properties.

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:49.030
While one EM wave must have an electric direction
and, therefore, must have a polarization

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:52.040
the beam is made of many of these waves.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:57.020
If their electric pieces kind of line up,
then we say the beam is polarized.

00:03:57.020 --> 00:04:01.640
But if they’re all oriented randomly, the
directions cancel and we can say the beam

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:03.270
is unpolarized.

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:06.660
The beam is what you get when you add all
those waves together.

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:11.470
It’s just easier to focus on individual
waves now and then try make predictions about

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:12.460
the beam later.

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:13.580
So let’s do that.

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:16.400
Consider this polarizer with this axis.

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:20.340
Suddenly a light wave comes in with an electric
part in this direction.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:24.440
The polarizer will still pass this portion
of that electric arrow.

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:28.639
That portion is smaller than the original,
so that wave comes out a little dimmer.

00:04:28.639 --> 00:04:34.020
If the beam passing through the polarizer
is unpolarized, like sunlight, then the same

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:37.870
amount is going to get blocked no matter how
the polarizer is turned.

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:40.260
That’s because you’ve got some waves like
this

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:41.380
and some waves like this

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:42.840
and some waves like this.

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:47.920
We say, on average, about 50% of the light
gets through, but, in practice, it’s more

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:50.740
like 38% because no polarizer is perfect.

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:53.280
Polarized sunglasses have another added benefit.

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:54.800
Glare Reduction!

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:58.120
You know, that obnoxious bright light from
things like cars.

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:00.100
Anyway, there’s a reason it happens.

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:02.480
Light comes in from the Sun and reflects.

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:07.280
Most of it scatters giving us color, but,
at angles around Brewster’s angle, the reflected

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:10.380
light is polarized parallel to the surface.

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:13.480
This light can create some very bright reflections

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:17.220
and it’s almost completely blocked by our polarized sunglasses.

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:22.100
That’s because the axis of your glasses
is perpendicular to the polarization of light.

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:25.620
You can see the same thing when two polarizers
are perpendicular.

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:30.220
The light from a lot of LCD screens is also 
polarized at 45 degrees,

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:33.080
so only half the light gets through your sunglasses.

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:37.280
If you turn the sunglasses, you
can make the screen appear darker or lighter.

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:41.480
So to summarize:
Polarized sunglasses use long chains of molecules

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:46.340
that can only vibrate in one direction,
to block a little over half of normal light

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:47.710
and almost all glare.

00:05:47.710 --> 00:05:49.520
Plus, they’re super stylish.

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:52.760
What’s the weirdest thing you’ve noticed
looking through polarized sunglasses?

